  "_comment": "Source list for M1 (docs/morphology.md). Each entry gives a standard irregular verb's base / past / past-participle. The linter flags the `past` and `pp` spellings when they appear in a World-English column; the suggested WoE form is computed from `base` by regularizeVerbPast() in src/morphology.ts. `pp` is omitted when identical to `past`. `homograph: true` marks a *standard-English* past/pp whose spelling collides with a valid everyday word (a noun, adjective, or direction) — those are demoted to low confidence so the default sweep does not false-positive on the non-verb sense; this is a forward-direction (SE-scanning) concern only. `reverseCollision: true` is a separate, reverse-direction flag: it marks a verb whose *WoE-regularized* form (e.g. `seed`, `hanged`) itself collides with a valid everyday word, so the reverse translator must not guess a restoration at all (#66) — most homographs (`speaked`, `bited`, `shooted`, `beared`) don't have this problem since their WoE forms are not real words. Zero-past verbs (their SE past is spelled exactly like the valid WoE base form, so the linter cannot detect them without part-of-speech context — those entries are `past` == `base`) are ALL included below (cost, put, hit, cut, set, let, read, shut, cast, spread, burst, hurt, bet, quit, split, bid — plus beat, whose past is zero but whose participle 'beaten' is distinct) purely for reverse-translator coverage (#67): the forward pipeline's zero-past auto-convert (translate.ts's zeroPastConvert) and the zero-past phrasal-head guard (core-lexicon.ts) both regularize these to non-standard coinages (quitted, putted, setted, …), so the reverse translator needs an entry to map each coinage back to the standard past. The linter itself still skips them (past == base = undetectable).",
